            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ver means “to see.” Its written v uses the Spanish sound you already know: it is not contrasted with a separate English-style v sound. Say ver."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: three present forms",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "present"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. present: veo.",
                "person: tú. present: ves.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. present: ve."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The tú and third-person forms resemble the familiar -er row. The yo form keeps an extra vowel: veo. Reuse known café: Veo café — “I see coffee.”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish ver continues Latin vidēre, “to see,” through Old Spanish veer. Loss of the consonant between vowels helps explain the shorter shape; the two adjacent vowels later contracted in the infinitive. English video belongs to the same Latin family, a memory bridge rather than a new Spanish word for this lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
